-- title:
--   $n=4$, p.25 — independent $\mathfrak{su}(1)$, $\mathfrak{su}(3)$, $\mathfrak{su}(2)$
-- statement:
--   For four qubits, the space splits into one $J=2$ irrep, three $J=1$ irreps and two $J=0$ irreps, and exchange acts as $\mathfrak{su}$ independently on each DFS:
--   1. $\dim\mathrm{DFS}_4(2)=1$, $\dim\mathrm{DFS}_4(1)=3$ and $\dim\mathrm{DFS}_4(0)=2$, at every legal $m$;
--   2. for every $J\in\{0,1,2\}$ and every legal $m$, every traceless Hermitian operator on $\mathrm{DFS}_4(J)$ agrees there with some element of $H^4_J$ (for $J=2$ this is $\mathfrak{su}(1)=0$).
--
--   This is the base case $n=4$ of the induction. The explicit operators (7.20)–(7.21) printed after the quoted sentence are false as printed ($[E_{12},E_{34}]=0$, and $Z=-E_{12}$ does not vanish on $J=2$) and are not part of the statement.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: we can independently perform su(1) (i.e. zero), su(3), and su(2) → SectorUniversal 4; reason: the Lie-algebra form of the claim, the same as the goal
--   - generalization: Hamiltonians obtained by scalar multiplication, addition, Lie-commutator and unitary conjugation → i X in the real Lie algebra generated by the i E_ab (laws 1–3 only); reason: the smaller closure is contained in the printed one, so the Lean statement specializes to it; conjugation adds nothing (milestone (4.6))
--   - generalization: m_J fixed (m_J = J in the proof) → every legal magnetic number m; reason: exchange commutes with the S_α (fact (i), (7.13)); the statement at m = J is a special case
--   - encoding: half-integer labels J, m → doubled labels j2 = 2J : ℕ, m2 = 2m : ℤ; reason: exact half-integers; same instances
-- source:
--   Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, Theory of Decoherence-Free Fault-Tolerant Universal Quantum Computation, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, §VII.C, p.25

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CollectiveDFS_SectorUniversal

open QAlgorithms

namespace CollectiveDFS

/-- Theorem 5 at `n = 4`, §VII.C (Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, p.25, PDF p.25): "For n = 4 the Hilbert space splits up
into one J = 2-irrep [DFS_4(2)], three J = 1-irreps [DFS_4(1)], and two J = 0-irreps
[DFS_4(0)] … Direct calculation of the effect of exchange on these DFSs shows that we can
independently perform su(1) (i.e. zero), su(3), and su(2)." That is:
(1) `dim DFS_4(2) = 1`, `dim DFS_4(1) = 3`, `dim DFS_4(0) = 2`, at every legal `m`;
(2) exchange acts as `su` independently on every `DFS_4(J)`, every legal `m`.

**Formalization Note** Doubled labels: `J = 2, 1, 0` are `4, 2, 0`. The displays (7.20) and (7.21)
that follow the quoted sentence are false as printed (`[E₁₂, E₃₄] = 0`, and `Z = −E₁₂` is not zero
on `J = 2`) and are not stated. -/
theorem base_four :
    (∀ m2 : ℤ, LegalM 4 m2 → Module.finrank ℂ (dfs 4 4 m2) = 1) ∧
    (∀ m2 : ℤ, LegalM 2 m2 → Module.finrank ℂ (dfs 4 2 m2) = 3) ∧
    Module.finrank ℂ (dfs 4 0 0) = 2 ∧
    SectorUniversal 4 := by sorry

end CollectiveDFS
